diagnosis_text:
I cannot reproduce the outage. Inter-VLAN connectivity between the desktop VLAN (172.16.10.0/24) and laptop VLAN (172.16.20.0/24) is working end-to-end. From PC4 (172.16.10.4) I can ping Laptop0 (172.16.20.10) with 0% loss, and the reverse direction also succeeds. The gateway “box that joins the two groups” is router0, which is routing between VLAN10 and VLAN20 via subinterfaces eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24). Both SVIs are up, and both endpoints have the correct default gateways configured (PC4 via 172.16.10.1; Laptop0 via 172.16.20.1). The successful pings with TTL=63 confirm a single L3 hop through router0, proving inter-VLAN routing is healthy.

Localization: router0’s eth0.10 and eth0.20 are operational; switching between PC4/laptop0 access ports on switch1 is also passing traffic. Router0’s routing table contains both local connected /24s and relevant next-hops for other networks, indicating no control-plane fault.

Root cause: No current fault found; the earlier symptom was likely transient or application-layer/DNS related rather than the inter-VLAN router failing.

Corrective fix: No changes required. Please retest your application. If the issue recurs, capture time-of-failure and we will check for intermittent trunk/SVI flaps or host firewall issues; but at present, the joining router (router0) is healthy and forwarding traffic correctly. 
